## 6. Conclusion

This thesis has presented a complete mathematical framework for the design of a compact, ultra‑high‑field NMR magnet. By rigorously coupling electromagnetic, 
mechanical, and thermal physics, and by incorporating state‑of‑the‑art mitigation strategies—constant contact resistivity, functionally graded overbanding, 
adjoint‑optimised pre‑emphasis, striation, and a conservatively bounded quench model—the AETHER design achieves 35.2 T, <1 ppm homogeneity, and <0.01 ppm/h drift
in a 10 mm bore. All claims are supported by proven error bounds or certified surrogate uncertainties. The tolerance analysis guarantees sub‑1 ppm homogeneity 
under worst‑case manufacturing variations. The detailed algorithms for the enhanced NSGA‑II and adjoint waveform optimization are fully documented,
ensuring reproducibility. While the work is purely theoretical, it provides a mathematically certified foundation that future experimental efforts can build upon,
with a clear roadmap for validation. The open mathematical formulation invites the community to extend and refine these results, bringing the vision of a benchtop 1.5 GHz NMR spectrometer closer to reality.

