## Appendix E: Sensitivity Analysis and Tolerance Budget

### E.1 Worst‑Case Homogeneity Bound from Adjoint Sensitivities

The first‑order sensitivity of the harmonic coefficient \(C_n\) to a geometric parameter \(\xi_i\) is \(\partial C_n/\partial\xi_i\), which is computed analytically from the Biot‑Savart derivatives. Assuming independent tolerances with maximum deviations \(\delta\xi_i = 3\sigma_i\), the worst‑case increase in homogeneity error is

\[
f_2^{\max} = f_2(0) + \sum_{n=2,4,6} \sum_i \left|\frac{\partial C_n}{\partial\xi_i}\right| 3\sigma_i\; R^n .
\]

For the Phase‑3 design and the tolerances listed below, this gives \(f_2^{\max}=0.97\) ppm, satisfying the 1 ppm requirement.

### E.2 Sobol Sensitivity Indices (Theoretical Prediction)

The total Sobol indices \(S_{T,i}\) are defined as the fraction of the variance of \(f_2\) contributed by parameter \(\xi_i\), including all interactions. In the linear approximation, \(S_{T,i} = (\partial f_2/\partial\xi_i)^2 p_i^2 / \sum_j (\partial f_2/\partial\xi_j)^2 p_j^2\). Using the analytic derivatives and the assumed manufacturing standard deviations, one obtains the values in Table E.1.

